  "dom_id": "step-q328557",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{3t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$",
  "worked_solution": "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{3t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $3$ units to the right.",
